    "created_at": "2026-08-10T09:37:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-10T09:37:34Z",
    "body": "> Changing document.open/write behavior without any telemetry data feels a bit risky.\n\nNote that the main effect would be on `document.write()` on documents created with XHR, or detached document streaming (`document.implementation.createHTMLDocument().write()`)\n\nSince the opt-in was mXSS protection (see [this discussion](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/831#issuecomment-714240292)), I don't know if telemetry would be super helpful as we might be opening a vector that's not exploited yet (making `<template shadowrootmode>` an active element in content injected via detached document streaming).\n\nHowever, I think this is not a worthwhile mXSS problem to tackle.\n- Downstream sanitizers usually don't stream anyway, so not ver useful in `document.write()`. They usually rely either on parsing with the browser into a fragment, or by parsing the whole fragment statically using a server side library.  They also don't use `document.write()` internally AFAICT.\n- Downstream sanitizers predominantly use allowlists (deny-by-default) of attributes rather than blocklist, so they won't allow `shadowrootmode` in the first place. \n\nSo to take advantage of this hypothetical mXSS vulnerability, there would have to be a combination of detached document streaming with a badly configured legacy sanitizer.\n\n\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
